# Nested Agents

## Overview

You want one Agent to run a team: a lead and a group of workers, sized by the
parent's state. In Jido, you declare that team in a `topology` block and the
Agent Server starts and supervises it.

This is where you'll end up:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
topology do
  agent :lead, Nested.Worker, initial_state: %{team: "lead"}

  agent :workers, Nested.Worker do
    count state(:size)
    initial_state %{team: state(:name)}
  end
end
```

This Livebook shows you how to declare members, bind counts and initial state
to the parent's state, check the plan without a process, and inspect the live
members. The terms are defined in
[Composition Vocabulary](composition-vocabulary.md).

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## Define A Member

A member is an ordinary Agent. It needs nothing special to run in a team.

```elixir
defmodule Nested.Worker do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "worker"

  agent do
    schema(
      Zoi.object(%{
        team: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.default("none"),
        done: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)
      })
    )
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/nested-agents")

    route "task.run", as: :run do
      action _input, name: "run_task", schema: Zoi.object(%{}), context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | done: context.agent_state.done + 1}}
      end
    end
  end
end
```

## Declare The Team

```elixir
defmodule Nested.Team do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "team"

  agent do
    schema(
      Zoi.object(%{
        name: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.default("alpha"),
        size: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(2)
      })
    )
  end

  topology do
    agent :lead, Nested.Worker, initial_state: %{team: "lead"}

    agent :workers, Nested.Worker do
      count state(:size)
      initial_state %{team: state(:name)}
    end
  end
end
```

Each `agent` entry declares one member:

- `:lead` has the default count of `1` and a literal initial state.
- `:workers` reads its count from the parent's `size` field and copies the
  parent's `name` into every worker.

`state/1` is a state binding. It refers to a field of the parent's committed
state. Jido resolves it when the parent has an instance.

Jido checks the topology when the module compiles. These fail the build:

- `state(:missing)` for a field the parent schema doesn't have;
- an initial state that doesn't match the member's schema;
- a member whose definition contains itself.

## Read The Plan

The topology is part of the definition, as plain data:

```elixir
Nested.Team.definition().topology.agents
|> Enum.map(&{&1.name, &1.count})
```

You get `[lead: 1, workers: %{key: :size, kind: :state}]`. The definition has
no state, so the `:workers` count is still a binding.

`Jido.Agent.Topology.bind/2` resolves bindings against a state map. It starts
nothing.

```elixir
team = Nested.Team.instantiate!(id: "team-1")
{:ok, members} = Jido.Agent.Topology.bind(team, team.state)

Enum.map(members, &{&1.name, &1.count, &1.state})
```

You get:

```text
[{:lead, 1, %{done: 0, team: "lead"}},
 {:workers, 2, %{done: 0, team: "alpha"}}]
```

Each member's state is complete: the member schema's defaults fill `done`.

Other state produces another team:

```elixir
beta = Nested.Team.instantiate!(id: "team-2", state: %{name: "beta", size: 3})
{:ok, members} = Jido.Agent.Topology.bind(beta, beta.state)

Enum.map(members, &{&1.name, &1.count, &1.state})
```

You get `:workers` with a count of `3` and `team: "beta"` in each slot.

A bound count must resolve to a positive integer. Jido checks this when you
create the instance:

```elixir
{:error, error} = Nested.Team.instantiate(id: "team-3", state: %{size: 0})

Exception.message(error)
```

The message is `"Expected a positive integer"`.

## Start The Team

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()
{:ok, server} = Jido.start_agent(Nested.Team, id: "team-1")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(server)
```

The Server binds the topology against the instance state and starts one Agent
Server per slot under its own activation.

Call `await_ready/2` before you use members. `start_agent/2` can return before
every slot is ready.

## Inspect The Children

`Jido.AgentServer.children/2` returns every child of the parent, keyed by tag:

```elixir
server
|> Jido.AgentServer.children()
|> Map.keys()
|> Enum.sort()
```

You get `["$topology/lead/1", "$topology/workers/1", "$topology/workers/2"]`.

Each value is `%{pid: pid, activation: root, placement: :owned}`. `:owned`
means the slot runs under the parent's activation and stops with the parent.

## Find Members By Name

`Jido.AgentServer.members/3` returns the PIDs for one member, in slot order:

```elixir
workers = Jido.AgentServer.members(server, :workers)

Enum.map(workers, fn pid ->
  agent = Jido.AgentServer.agent(pid)
  {agent.id, agent.state}
end)
```

You get:

```text
[{"team-1/$topology/workers/1", %{done: 0, team: "alpha"}},
 {"team-1/$topology/workers/2", %{done: 0, team: "alpha"}}]
```

A member ID is the parent ID plus the slot tag. Members are registered in the
instance Registry, so `Jido.whereis_agent/1` finds them by ID:

```elixir
Jido.whereis_agent("team-1/$topology/workers/1") == hd(workers)
```

You get `true`.

## Call One Member

Each slot is a full Agent Server:

```elixir
run = Nested.Worker.run_signal!()
{:ok, worker, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(hd(workers), run)

{worker.state, Jido.AgentServer.status(server).state_version}
```

You get `{%{done: 1, team: "alpha"}, 0}`. The worker committed revision 1 of
its own state. The parent's revision is still `0`. State never merges across
the team.

## Stop The Team

Stopping the parent stops its activation and every owned slot:

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent("team-1")

Enum.map(workers, &Process.alive?/1)
```

You get `[false, false]`.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

## Summary

- A `topology` block declares members as definition data. It starts nothing.
- `count` and `initial_state` take literals or `state/1` bindings.
- `Jido.Agent.Topology.bind/2` resolves bindings without a process.
- The Server starts one Agent Server per slot under its activation.
- `children/2` lists slots by tag. `members/3` returns PIDs by name.
- Each member owns its own state and revision.

